@@ -0,0 +1,50 @@
+# Launch pane fix — result report
+
+## What shipped
+
+- `hive-launch` now allocates an existing-session window with tmux append/free-slot
+  semantics (`new-window -a`) while retaining the task name as the window registry.
+- Window-creation failure is fail-safe: the launcher leaves the assigned board state,
+  clones, and wrapper intact, then prints a filled kickoff and exact manual completion
+  commands. It explicitly says not to re-run `hive-launch`.
+- The tooling drill reproduces the old occupied-index collision, checks the resulting
+  stranded `assigned` state, exercises the half-launch recovery print, and keeps its
+  fake-tmux `PATH` scoped to that one invocation.
+
+## How verified
+
+Before the fix, the reproduction targeted `session:4` after index 4 was occupied;
+the explicit target failed, leaving the task launch path without a usable pane. The
+after-state uses the same occupied-index setup and verifies that the named task window
+is allocated by append/free-slot semantics, while the injected window failure leaves
+`drill-half-launch` `assigned` and prints the filled kickoff/manual completion path.
+
+Static verification passed:
+
+- `bash -n scripts/hive-launch scripts/hive-tooling-drill.sh`
+- `shellcheck -x scripts/hive-launch scripts/hive-tooling-drill.sh`
+
+The end-to-end drill is `scripts/hive-tooling-drill.sh`; it requires the project’s
+Podman/Postgres stack and tmux access to be running. In this review sandbox the drill
+was attempted but stopped at tmux session creation with `Operation not permitted`;
+that is an environment restriction, before any drill assertion ran. The deterministic
+static checks above passed, and the drill contains the reproduction and after-state
+assertions described here.
+
+## To operate
+
+The ordering choice is deliberate: provision the clones, wrapper, and board assignment
+before creating the pane, then use `tmux new-window -a` for an existing session. This
+preserves the durable assignment and avoids choosing a possibly occupied numeric
+window index. If pane creation fails, do not re-run `hive-launch`. Follow the stderr
+block: run its `new-window -a` command (or its `new-session` alternative), verify the
+task-named window, and use the filled kickoff. Use `task.reassigned` only if the
+recovered worker is genuinely dead; see the RUNBOOK half-launch section above.
+
+## Reflection
+
+The collision was a numeric-index allocation problem, while the more dangerous
+failure was the ordering gap that could strand a durable assignment without an
+operator path. Naming the tmux window remains the registry; append allocation removes
+the index collision, and the explicit recovery print plus RUNBOOK guidance makes the
+remaining half-launch state recoverable without duplicating the task.
